P^{\prime}$; the point $Q$ goes to $Q^{\prime}$. Then rotate $(∆M^{\prime
}NP^{\prime
})$ clockwise $270°$ around $M$ to get $(∆MP^{\prime
}N^{\prime
})$. The point $Q$ goes to the new position $(Q^{\prime})^{\prime}
$. </think> <answer>$(-4, 11)$ Maths Hint from User Here is a hint for your next problem: If $P$ and $Q$ are points in the plane, can you find the matrix that gets $P$ to $Q$? Good luck, and notice we have grades on the clock! ?</answer>

